# Stripe Billing — Product Catalog & Usage-Based Billing

## Overall Role and Context

I worked as a **Senior Full-Stack Engineer within Stripe Billing**, primarily across the **Usage-Based Billing** and **Product Catalog & Pricing** domains.

My work focused on three major areas:

1. **Meter Event Ingestion** — accepting very high volumes of customer usage data reliably.
2. **Stream Processing & Metering** — processing, reconciling, and aggregating usage events into billable quantities.
3. **Flexible Pricing & Rate Cards** — allowing businesses to configure flat-rate, tiered, usage-based, and hybrid pricing without custom billing implementations.

These systems were part of a broader billing lifecycle:

```text
Product Catalog
      │
      ▼
Pricing / Rate Cards
      │
      ▼
Customer Subscription / Contract
      │
      │
Usage Events
      ▼
Meter Event Ingestion
      │
      ▼
Event Stream
      │
      ▼
Stream Processing
      │
      ▼
Usage Aggregation
      │
      ▼
Rating / Pricing
      │
      ▼
Invoice Line Items
      │
      ▼
Invoice → Payment → Revenue
```

I collaborated closely with **product managers, engineering managers, product designers, infrastructure/platform engineers, SREs, data engineers, and engineers responsible for subscriptions, invoicing, payments, credits, and revenue systems**.

The main architectural goal was to separate **high-volume usage collection** from **financially authoritative billing**, allowing each layer to scale independently while preserving the correctness expected from a financial platform.

Stripe has publicly described a similar high-level architecture for Usage-Based Billing: stateless event ingestion into an event bus, Apache Flink-based processing, active-active regional processing, and separate fast and authoritative aggregation paths. Stripe reports supporting up to **100,000 usage events per second per business** in this architecture.


# Project 1 — Meter Event Ingestion Service

## Business Context and Strategic Motivation

Usage-based pricing became increasingly important as SaaS, infrastructure, and AI companies moved away from purely fixed subscriptions.

Instead of charging:

```text
Pro Plan
$99 / month
```

businesses increasingly needed to charge for things such as:

```text
API requests
AI tokens
GPU seconds
Compute hours
Storage consumed
Messages processed
Successful transactions
```

This changes the scale of Billing dramatically.

A subscription might generate one billing operation per month, while an AI customer can generate **millions or billions of usage events** during the same period.

The existing synchronous API pattern was not ideal for that workload because each request could require authentication, routing, RPC calls, business validation, and database access before responding.

The objective of the project was therefore to create an ingestion layer that could:

- accept extremely high event volumes;
- respond quickly to clients;
- isolate downstream failures from producers;
- prevent data loss;
- support retries and deduplication;
- evolve event schemas safely;
- protect the platform from abusive or noisy tenants.

Stripe publicly describes the same strategic problem: its second-generation UBB API was designed to increase throughput roughly **100×**, using stateless validation at the edge followed by asynchronous processing through an event bus.


## My Role

I owned a significant portion of the design and implementation of the **Meter Event Ingestion Service**.

My responsibilities included:

- defining ingestion API contracts;
- designing asynchronous event routing;
- establishing schema and validation rules;
- implementing batching and backpressure controls;
- defining idempotency and deduplication strategies;
- working with the stream-processing team on the event contract;
- defining SLIs/SLOs for ingestion;
- coordinating rollout and compatibility with existing Billing APIs;
- working with Dashboard/API teams on asynchronous failure visibility.

The core implementation stack was:

```text
Go
Kafka
Protobuf
gRPC
```

Go was used for the high-concurrency stateless service layer, while Protobuf provided a strongly typed event contract. Kafka acted as the durable asynchronous boundary between ingestion and processing. gRPC was primarily appropriate for internal service communication rather than as a requirement for the public-facing API.


