```

For an interview, I would use personally verified project numbers.

As broader Stripe platform context—not as a personal accomplishment—Stripe publicly reported a UBB capability of **100,000 events/sec per business**, a target of **99.999% availability**, and zero-data-loss requirements for the redesign.


## Most Technically Challenging Part

The hardest problem was not simply handling a high number of HTTP requests.

The real challenge was preserving **financial reliability while decoupling ingestion from processing**.

Once a customer receives a successful acknowledgment, losing that usage event can eventually translate directly into incorrect revenue.

We therefore had to reason carefully about:

```text
API acknowledgement
        ↓
durable persistence boundary
        ↓
retries
        ↓
duplicate events
        ↓
stream replay
        ↓
event ordering
        ↓
billing correctness
```

Multi-tenant load was another major challenge because a single large AI or infrastructure customer could generate orders of magnitude more traffic than the typical merchant.


## What I Learned / What I Would Do Differently

The biggest lesson was that an asynchronous API cannot be treated as merely a faster synchronous API.

It needs a first-class operational experience.

If I were designing it again, I would invest earlier in:

- event tracing from ingestion through aggregation;
- merchant-visible processing states;
- standardized rejection reason codes;
- per-account lag and throughput dashboards;
- replay tooling;
- automated hot-partition detection.

I would also define the durability boundary and idempotency semantics extremely early because those decisions affect almost every downstream service.


