# Project 2 — Stream Processing Service

## Business Context and Strategic Motivation

Once the system could ingest extremely high event volumes, the next challenge was converting those events into trustworthy usage data.

Meter events can arrive:

```text
late
duplicated
out of order
replayed
after temporary failures
from multiple geographic regions
```

A normal analytics pipeline can often tolerate some delay or inconsistency.

A billing pipeline cannot.

If we aggregate incorrectly:

```text
Actual usage       10,000,000 units

Calculated usage    9,700,000 units
```

we may underbill.

If we process an event twice:

```text
Actual usage       10,000,000 units

Calculated usage   10,300,000 units
```

we may overbill.

Both outcomes damage customer trust.

The objective was to create a distributed stream-processing layer that could provide both **low-latency usage visibility** and **financially reliable authoritative results**.


## My Role

I worked on the design and implementation of the distributed processing pipeline using:

```text
Java
Apache Flink
Kafka
RocksDB
```

My ownership included:

- Flink job design;
- event-time and watermark strategy;
- stateful processing;
- checkpoint and recovery behavior;
- late-event handling;
- partition/key design;
- replay behavior;
- operational metrics;
- integration with ingestion and aggregation services;
- failure testing;
- production rollout.

I worked particularly closely with engineers responsible for ingestion, billing aggregation, alerts/credits, invoicing, infrastructure, and reliability.


## Overall Architecture

```text
                  Kafka
                    │
                    ▼
          ┌───────────────────┐
          │ Apache Flink      │
          │ Java              │
          │                   │
          │ Deserialize       │
          │ Normalize         │
          │ Deduplicate       │
          │ Assign event time │
          │ Enrich            │
          │ Partition         │
          └─────────┬─────────┘
                    │
             Stateful processing
                    │
                    ▼
                 RocksDB
                    │
          ┌─────────┴───────────┐
          │                     │
          ▼                     ▼
      Fast Path          Authoritative Path
          │                     │
   near-real-time            durable
      aggregates            aggregation
          │                     │
          ▼                     ▼
 Alerts / balance       Rating / invoicing
 visibility             financial records
```

Stripe publicly describes a similar dual-path model.

Its fast pipeline uses approximately **30-second tumbling windows** for time-sensitive functionality such as alerts and credit balances.

Its slower path uses approximately **five-minute windows**, writes events durably, and handles delayed/out-of-order events before producing invoicing, analytics, and financial data.


## Why Apache Flink

Flink was particularly well suited because the problem required more than basic message consumption.

We needed:

```text
event-time semantics
stateful transformations
distributed checkpoints
large keyed state
watermarks
windowing
failure recovery
horizontal scaling
```

A simplified pipeline was:

```text
Kafka Source
    ↓
deserialize
    ↓
assign timestamp / watermark
    ↓
keyBy(account, customer, meter)
    ↓
deduplicate
    ↓
stateful processing
    ↓
window / aggregate
    ↓
downstream sink
```

RocksDB provided a practical state backend for large keyed state that could not safely remain entirely on the JVM heap.


## Major Architecture Tradeoffs

### Processing time vs. event time

Processing time is simpler:

```text
process event when server receives it
```

but usage events can arrive late.

For example:

```text
Event timestamp     10:00:01
Arrival time         10:08:42
```

For billing, the event usually needs to belong to the billing period corresponding to **10:00:01**, not 10:08:42.

We therefore favored event-time semantics.

The downside is considerably more complexity around watermarks and how long to wait for delayed events.


### Fast results vs. authoritative results

One pipeline cannot optimize perfectly for both.

Customers want usage dashboards and credit alerts quickly.

Financial systems want maximum correctness.

We therefore conceptually separated:

```text
FAST

short windows
low latency
alerts
usage visibility
```

from:

```text
AUTHORITATIVE

longer reconciliation window
durable state
late-event handling
invoice correctness
```

Trying to force financial finality into the low-latency path would either increase latency or make edge cases harder to handle.


### Large state vs. memory performance

Keeping state entirely in memory is fast, but a billing system can maintain state across:

```text
millions of customers
×
multiple meters
×
billing periods
×
pricing dimensions
```

That can create enormous state.

RocksDB introduced additional local I/O and operational complexity but provided a much safer scaling model for large keyed state.


## Delivery

**Phase 1 — baseline Flink pipeline**

We built ingestion-to-processing integration and established the basic event contract.

**Phase 2 — state and event time**

We introduced keyed state, event timestamps, watermarks, deduplication, and late-event handling.

**Phase 3 — resilience**

We tested worker failures, Kafka rebalances, checkpoint restoration, poison events, replay, and regional degradation.

**Phase 4 — production migration**

Rather than immediately replacing existing results, the new pipeline could run in shadow mode and compare outputs against the existing source of truth.

Differences were categorized and reconciled before progressively moving production consumers.


## Success Metrics

Metrics I would expect to track include:

```text
Events processed/sec
P95 event-processing latency
Consumer lag
Checkpoint duration
Checkpoint failure rate
State size
Recovery time
Late-event percentage
Duplicate percentage
Fast vs authoritative aggregate divergence
Regional reconciliation discrepancies
End-to-end usage-to-rated-output latency
```

Stripe publicly reports **P95 latency below 30 seconds for time-sensitive UBB operations** and approximately **five minutes from ingestion to rated output for most use cases**. These are useful platform benchmarks but should not be presented as personal metrics unless they were directly tied to your work.


## Most Technically Challenging Part

The hardest technical problem was **distributed state correctness under failure**.

An event can be:

```text
read from Kafka
       ↓
state updated
       ↓
worker crashes
       ↓
checkpoint restored
       ↓
event replayed
```

If the pipeline is not designed correctly, the same usage can be counted twice.

We had to understand the relationship between:

```text
Kafka offsets
Flink checkpoints
RocksDB state
sink commits
replay semantics
```

Another difficult problem was late data.

A billing platform cannot simply discard an event because it arrived outside a normal analytics window.

The architecture needed controlled mechanisms to incorporate delayed events while still eventually finalizing invoiceable usage.


## What I Learned / What I Would Do Differently

The biggest lesson was that streaming correctness is largely about **state management**, not message transport.

If I revisited the project, I would:

- establish replay as a first-class production workflow earlier;
- build automated aggregate comparison tooling from the beginning;
- create clearer state-size budgets per meter/customer;
- instrument checkpoint health more aggressively;
- define explicit lateness policies by use case rather than applying one universal watermark strategy.

I would also separate the operational expectations of the fast and authoritative paths earlier so teams would not accidentally treat near-real-time state as financially final.


