# Project 3 — Flexible Pricing & Rate Card Service

## Business Context and Strategic Motivation

The metering system answers:

> **How much did the customer use?**

The pricing system answers:

> **What should that usage cost?**

Modern SaaS and AI companies increasingly need more than a simple monthly subscription.

They might combine:

```text
$1,000 monthly platform fee

+

$20 per seat

+

10 million included tokens

+

$0.001 per additional token

+

volume discount above 100M tokens
```

Enterprise customers might additionally negotiate:

```text
custom rate
minimum commitment
special tier
effective date
customer-specific discount
```

Historically, companies often implemented this logic directly in application code.

That makes pricing changes slow and risky because every new business model requires engineering work.

The strategic goal of the Flexible Pricing and Rate Card project was to turn pricing into **configuration rather than custom code**.

This aligns with Stripe's current Billing strategy. Stripe now emphasizes usage/hybrid models, multidimensional pricing, commits, bespoke contracts, and increasingly large product catalogs through Stripe Billing and Metronome.


## My Role

I worked as a senior full-stack engineer across both the merchant-facing experience and pricing backend.

The primary stack was:

```text
Ruby on Rails
PostgreSQL
React
TypeScript
```

I owned or contributed to:

- pricing/rate-card domain modeling;
- API design;
- pricing versioning;
- effective dates;
- validation rules;
- pricing preview;
- React pricing configuration flows;
- migration of existing price configurations;
- integration with metering/rating systems;
- testing and rollout.

I partnered closely with product management and design because pricing configuration is both a technical and UX problem.

I also worked with Subscription, Usage Billing, Invoice, Contracts, and Revenue teams because pricing definitions ultimately affect all of those systems.


